#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b0dbd2f9-8fef-5af8-b4ce-a371e65c6441" :question/math-academy-id "e-21830" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x^3 - x + 5\\cos x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-\\pi,\\pi).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in(-\\pi,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/assets/766b5034dc8f2dd6f3eb8e87f22f5156f58e9ab3aaebd90fa8770d6912d46e67.png)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "43229a59-0b01-55ff-8b10-b1238360d277" :question/math-academy-id "e-21831" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/assets/ee53aca390d2803fa92f3239dc7548792c6be9e4c05a102fe936aeb9e7ced6d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-6x-2$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi (-6x-2) \\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-6x-2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(-6x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{6}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-6\\pi -2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{6}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0-\\frac{6}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim (-2-3\\pi) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}} \n\\cdot \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\cos(nx)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "c8b4459b-e0ed-56cc-ab06-5be09baa7363" :question/math-academy-id "e-21833" :question/problem "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_0.$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9caae9bd-2c49-5f48-a7be-388add7e9b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{2}+2|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357733} {:answer/id #uuid "fac0e189-fd8a-5927-88f1-c638cbaafdd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357734} {:answer/id #uuid "511d5a2c-171c-591b-88a1-3b9de859c8c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357735} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c04153-37ed-583e-a766-f15e63d5efe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357736} {:answer/id #uuid "0b7961b7-4eb1-590b-a8bf-7edf3da68cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{2}+2x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "511d5a2c-171c-591b-88a1-3b9de859c8c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357735} :answer-field/id #uuid "39ad1ac1-6653-57f9-bb67-54f4ef83bd12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcf50127-6a15-5bfa-b137-f8e9738bae25" :question/math-academy-id "q-340794" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}-2(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f47c6e38-7293-5663-a864-ddaf830aea11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi^2 + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f47c6e38-7293-5663-a864-ddaf830aea11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi^2 + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232893} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f391935-24b1-5be2-9327-d8c7c663cf06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d95b499-c990-5e2e-96ad-0c45f0c2fce8" :question/math-academy-id "q-340814" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[{x}^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }({\\pi }^{3}+2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2({\\pi }^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d43740-1c1b-503f-bc0d-76f481969199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3/\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232894} {:answer/id #uuid "159e41ba-05c9-57ed-8082-c794ef2adfb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06d43740-1c1b-503f-bc0d-76f481969199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3/\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232894} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9c962d-3866-5a8a-aee0-f84a45e2caa8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb68e665-73e2-503c-a3f5-1fe023348ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-340815" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{3}{2}-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1c37179-f214-594f-8ae6-8608c703868b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357741} {:answer/id #uuid "f59fa017-52f1-5a7a-9648-5eedaaf628f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357742} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbdff3d-d48a-502a-8094-2c4e10972910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357743} {:answer/id #uuid "dab6dac3-20f0-5ee3-a15a-6178ad7fb0aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{3}+x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357744} {:answer/id #uuid "9da00bb1-e26d-5682-b059-5e099c4e683e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{3}+|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f59fa017-52f1-5a7a-9648-5eedaaf628f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357742} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fcb1bfb-fc44-5a07-a562-acee7617b6b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fa2f0b6-b645-57f3-bffa-8b74f5318044" :question/math-academy-id "q-341240" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bfc3eb5-fd28-5c4e-8411-43911f9ba01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357748} {:answer/id #uuid "8a723dd0-6d1f-5e3c-85b6-cdd320226be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|x{|}^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357749} {:answer/id #uuid "42aaa73c-039f-5e8e-bf02-2c5a9fa69d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357750} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfa2512-1bc6-5b6c-80de-7542d1a3920f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357751} {:answer/id #uuid "571be2a9-3753-5f0b-a015-b83f903361d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42aaa73c-039f-5e8e-bf02-2c5a9fa69d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357750} :answer-field/id #uuid "7577994b-1cbd-51e0-8937-750aa7698882" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f88a83b4-0e18-5fc8-8f06-28f6b0873a88" :question/math-academy-id "q-341243" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1550ff51-2576-5191-a575-99a05d0b6c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1550ff51-2576-5191-a575-99a05d0b6c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357755} :answer-field/id #uuid "3acf4f31-0516-52d2-b4ba-475e5f46760c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45a412fd-5ad8-5ab2-be4e-40cd61e06f60" :question/math-academy-id "q-341251" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=(2x+1){e}^{2x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is the value of $f(-2)?$\n\n$f(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(2(-x)+1){e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & =(-2x+1){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =(-2(-2)+1){e}^{-2(-2)}=5{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "818acf01-a016-5ead-adab-9399400e0bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357758} {:answer/id #uuid "260bdf96-c603-5409-b119-c346fb118d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "260bdf96-c603-5409-b119-c346fb118d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357759} :answer-field/id #uuid "c233d74d-d8e9-5b86-a325-3b8d6847524f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28d117cf-c663-562b-8f71-9a6082166c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-341255" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+2x+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is the value of $f(-3)?$\n\n$f(-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}+2(-x)+3 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}-2(-3)+3=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cc98c6-6295-5393-a3de-78fcda678260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357762} {:answer/id #uuid "992a1001-628b-5d74-ad00-fcd704cfd3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357763} {:answer/id #uuid "ad14f439-3195-5530-8e59-800c39ca0349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{20(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357764} {:answer/id #uuid "db3ce0e6-bc2c-5748-9079-0378d65a36b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357765} {:answer/id #uuid "0b689931-ae56-50a8-b29b-4d9e4d912891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11cc98c6-6295-5393-a3de-78fcda678260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357762} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b9b8f79-d0d6-5632-aea8-e858f26c5e30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2b04afa-d57d-5dc2-8054-36c00f74d55f" :question/math-academy-id "q-341259" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=10x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=10x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{20}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{20}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{20((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "569ee114-3ff4-5189-a346-598bfc7dbb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357769} {:answer/id #uuid "b2258be3-9503-5e6d-a82a-e8311e9f3d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357770} {:answer/id #uuid "97046c78-10c7-5ea0-9dad-971c536953a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357771} {:answer/id #uuid "23cd940d-a462-574e-9c5d-2384ddb31aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "569ee114-3ff4-5189-a346-598bfc7dbb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357769} :answer-field/id #uuid "5206d921-9c01-5156-ba6b-1512de241e2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f37a0aae-8786-5218-a5a4-6ceb25fd3a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357774} {:answer/id #uuid "26a012c5-b9e6-59ee-b119-5e320e4c57c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357775} {:answer/id #uuid "78578f24-b585-570f-aa8d-8c70205a7c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357776} {:answer/id #uuid "ed224253-32af-5dac-bd5f-6c132b0dbab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78578f24-b585-570f-aa8d-8c70205a7c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357776} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee333deb-1663-5dfe-9276-2f03046ee524" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a6c8b4c-d663-5855-bad2-c5ea86bef0a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-341265" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=5x+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(2+\\frac{5}{2}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=5x+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(5x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{5}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((5\\pi +2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{5}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{5}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{10((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(2+\\frac{5}{2}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e65f3ed7-09c4-5a34-90f8-47303b407d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357780} {:answer/id #uuid "6a529e7f-2e08-5d32-86be-26c51241e5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357781} {:answer/id #uuid "563aa707-66eb-5e1a-abc3-4ef1a2ebc480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357782} {:answer/id #uuid "643ec4f7-c8db-50a5-9ad2-42fae68fea65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "563aa707-66eb-5e1a-abc3-4ef1a2ebc480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357782} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3b4b0b-4d99-58dc-989a-53d818e7501f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357779} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fd8c29f-10d5-59cf-92c4-8e9d835484b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357785} {:answer/id #uuid "024aa47a-05d8-5c9d-a30d-3cecbbb42bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357786} {:answer/id #uuid "2901cd18-615f-5f9d-921c-d92a22ca52ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357787} {:answer/id #uuid "12f283f9-3139-53b5-b694-bd3c9def08c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2901cd18-615f-5f9d-921c-d92a22ca52ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357787} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd32b3c-c352-5793-87a1-07170bf3406a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8ee4544-292e-57b2-955f-27993ad05085" :question/math-academy-id "q-341266" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x+4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=x+4$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((\\pi +4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)-(0+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx))+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a02ab56c-314a-55af-87cf-92ce8ab8cee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357791} {:answer/id #uuid "f6004338-463b-588d-967d-295d822a44c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357792} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc66baa-cee1-5e97-bc42-c743a81ee72e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357793} {:answer/id #uuid "3add20d7-d24a-56e9-9292-a3e232fe084f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a02ab56c-314a-55af-87cf-92ce8ab8cee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357791} :answer-field/id #uuid "95ed7960-a5dc-5630-8a2f-a63797c02013" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0768ccb5-b52c-50aa-8be0-b200256c8eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357796} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc548be-a684-5d56-893a-4b4c7cc83a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357797} {:answer/id #uuid "e79d5c80-da53-51c8-84b8-9de2ca6f08eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357798} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf98b43-db4e-56fd-94f3-7058a6e08a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e79d5c80-da53-51c8-84b8-9de2ca6f08eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357798} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7392ee-bfd4-550c-a433-adaab877cd63" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a16f0f8b-2c48-53b0-a87f-d492038e6e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-341267" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=3x+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(1+\\frac{3}{2}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=3x+1$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(3x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{3}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((3\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{3}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{3}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{6((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(1+\\frac{3}{2}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aec43d1e-b788-5251-b141-651412a7be3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357802} {:answer/id #uuid "766c98b8-c1a3-534b-b9cb-af0f93c308b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357803} {:answer/id #uuid "db3f01a0-0439-5777-afc9-9514983997b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357804} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7731a6-b074-5341-b367-eb4f4ff6df64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f7731a6-b074-5341-b367-eb4f4ff6df64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357805} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea0d337-1a83-5a20-86cd-d0e8ff50378e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357801} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c2887ee-02a6-5d72-972b-b70d8600218e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357807} {:answer/id #uuid "76740c7d-6aef-5f9a-98ab-453559ec7d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357808} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb3c9f7-d44c-5b25-9801-c68dc701d260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357809} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaaad89-efec-5b51-b69d-ae1a96209b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eb3c9f7-d44c-5b25-9801-c68dc701d260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357809} :answer-field/id #uuid "476f726c-4945-5377-9515-8ee23e68d8ed" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4db222ac-c7bb-54f6-9ac9-8f6851becd78" :question/math-academy-id "q-341269" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-2x+5$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(5-\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-2x+5$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-2x+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-2x+5\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(-2x+5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{2}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-2\\pi +5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{2}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0-\\frac{2}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(5-\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69311e12-d8a1-5d89-8d6d-fdea5625fb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\pi^2}{3}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69311e12-d8a1-5d89-8d6d-fdea5625fb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\pi^2}{3}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357813} :answer-field/id #uuid "18279a36-95ad-512d-a4fe-442dd9f0abee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48fa455a-c17a-5a1a-bbec-c5380e59e04b" :question/math-academy-id "q-341276" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=5{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=5{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(5{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{3}}{3}+2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c16995a6-9123-56cc-9c39-7e4dd9712b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi}(\\sqrt{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c16995a6-9123-56cc-9c39-7e4dd9712b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi}(\\sqrt{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357816} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb0ce0d7-449d-59e8-956f-34f9ed7421c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b98e7a19-83c6-5116-a206-cf18bc4eba0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341283" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6e99d2-e79d-530f-96f4-168eedcbd583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\pi}(\\sqrt{3}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357819} {:answer/id #uuid "d293d0d2-fd7f-585e-80a7-161f1d6bd91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e6e99d2-e79d-530f-96f4-168eedcbd583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\pi}(\\sqrt{3}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357819} :answer-field/id #uuid "93f221f8-a204-567e-a49e-90389dfc1efe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c50dd619-ff30-5a53-b1c0-0b59df435810" :question/math-academy-id "q-341285" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
